 ====== Leetcode 118. Pascal's Triangle =======

Step 1: Create an empty list that will store all rows of Pascal’s Triangle.
Step 2: If the number of rows requested is 0, return the empty list.
Step 3: Create the first row containing only one element, which is 1.
Step 4: Add this first row to the main list.
Step 5: If the number of rows requested is 1, return the list because the triangle is complete.
Step 6: Start a loop from the second row up to the required number of rows.
Step 7: For each new row, create a new empty list.
Step 8: Add 1 as the first element of the new row.
Step 9: Get the previous row from the main list.
Step 10: For the middle elements of the new row, add numbers by summing two adjacent numbers from the previous row.
Step 11: Continue this process until all middle elements are calculated.
Step 12: Add 1 as the last element of the new row.
Step 13: Add the completed row to the main list.
Step 14: Repeat this process until all rows are generated.
Step 15: Return the main list containing all rows of Pascal’s Triangle.


Let n = number of rows.
Time Complexity:
Row 1 has 1 element
Row 2 has 2 elements
Row 3 has 3 elements
Row 4 has 4 elements
...
Row n has n elements
So total elements created are:
1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n
That means we are doing work for all these elements.
If n = 5
Work = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15
If n = 100
Work = 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 100
This grows roughly like n × n.
So Time Complexity = O(n²)
Now Space Complexity:
We are storing all these numbers in memory.
Total numbers stored are also:
1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n
That also grows like n × n.
So Space Complexity = O(n²)
In simple words:
Because we create and store around n² numbers,
both time and space are O(n²).



      ----------------> code  <--------------
class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> al=new ArrayList<>();
    public List<List<Integer>> generate(int numRows) {
        if(numRows==0)return al;

        List<Integer> arr=new ArrayList<>();
        arr.add(1);
        al.add(arr);
        if(numRows==1)return al;

        for(int i=1;i<numRows;i++){
            List<Integer> arr1=new ArrayList<>();
            arr1.add(1);

            List<Integer> prev=al.get(i-1);
            for(int j=1;j<i;j++){
                arr1.add(prev.get(j-1)+prev.get(j));
            }
            arr1.add(1);

            al.add(arr1);
        }
        return al;
    }
}
